host Vultr SEA NixOS VPS ai-jail pier (Daylight DC-1 hand) -- Eastern (EDT)
title a refusal that must stop the lap
prompt Closing the invariant round -- and the row bound refused a third time in four commits.
think The 192-byte row bound refused three rows and was right every time. Twice the commit shipped anyway, because the row-writing step and the commit were separate commands rather than one chain.
think The bound has no way to reach the work that follows it. When a check runs before a write, the caller owns the refusal -- that is the transferable rule, narrower than chain your commands.
think Three arrivals in four commits is a lantern firing repeatedly, so it becomes a loom rather than a resolution to be more careful.
obs Counted first: 41 flat logs, zero unnamed by the pin, after repairing the two by hand. The guard gates that at zero and joins the roster as the 54th, so the next cold open catches it rather than whoever reads a line of output.
obs A folded log passes free by named rule -- its row moved to its day's immutable shelf when its file did, and index_fold already holds that pairing. This watches the flat room, which is the one a lap appends to.
obs Proved the three a careless check gets wrong: one missing among three found rather than carried by a majority, a substring refused so _one.kyri cannot answer for _one-more.kyri, and an absent pin refused rather than read as zero logs and called clean.
loom witness=log_has_a_row behaviors=12 flat_logs=41 missing=0 guards=54
file tools/fixtures/log_has_a_row_scan.sh every flat log named by the pin, gated at zero
file tools/fixtures/log_has_a_row_control.sh twelve behaviors on planted rooms in a throwaway pen
file tools/l/log_has_a_row_witness.rish the standing guard
file construction/REDS.md row %209 booked; %207-%208 folded
file construction/standing-equipment.kyri log_has_a_row seated; the roster stands at 54
recommend check-in (checkpoint) pond holds 43% of the invariant gap and pond/apps/drawn_terminal.rye is 312 asserts with none covered -- a sweep that size wants Keaton's word on where to start
